| Motion | Inspect timing, interruption, gestures, reduced motion, and performance. Implement when changes are requested; report findings when the request is an audit. |

Mixed requests can combine modes. An audit alone does not authorize fixes. A normal build or polish request already authorizes scoped reversible edits; do not add a separate approval step.

## Work in a focused loop

1. Inspect existing behavior and states.
2. Select the smallest useful pattern and state its purpose: feedback, spatial continuity, hierarchy, or disclosure.
3. Implement within scope using platform-native components and existing project conventions.
4. Render the result and exercise relevant states, including repeated input and reduced motion.
5. Fix observed issues. Report what changed, verification performed, and unavailable checks.

Keep persistence and business actions independent of animation callbacks. An interruption or zero-duration animation must not change whether an action succeeds. Preserve input and visible errors when an action fails.

Translate browser ideas deliberately: hover becomes touch feedback; DOM focus becomes native accessibility and appropriate focus behavior; CSS and Motion values become native configuration. Tune web and native springs separately. Screenshots establish appearance; recordings and device interaction establish motion quality. Static scans cannot prove smoothness.

Use project gallery/check/capture commands when present. Do not install a second skill or a hosted service merely to fetch reference guidance. Do not copy a complete upstream catalog. Preserve notices for code actually incorporated, and verify its license.

Record accepted project-specific decisions in DESIGN.md when implementation work changes the design foundation. Change this global skill only for lessons that apply across projects and are supported by observed behavior.
